  "ch6.unit.first0": "with the first dose",
  "ch6.say.running": "The curve is running; statistics for the last day will be given after six days.",
  "ch6.say.inside": "The last day stays inside the window the whole time: plateau peak {pk}, trough {tr}, both between {lo} and {hi}.",
  "ch6.say.both": "The peak {pk} rises above the upper edge {hi}, and the trough {tr} falls below the lower edge {lo}: within a day there are times of too much and times of too little.",
  "ch6.say.over": "The trough {tr} is within the window, but the peak {pk} exceeds the upper edge {hi}.",
  "ch6.say.below": "The peak {pk} is within the window, but the trough {tr} is below the lower edge {lo}: each day there is a stretch when there is not enough drug.",
  "ch6.say.allover": "With a half-life of {half} hours, even the plateau trough {tr} is above the upper edge {hi}: it is above the window all day.",
  "ch6.say.late": "The first crossing of the upper edge comes at hour {first}, and only after that does it settle on the plateau (peak {pk}, trough {tr}). When clearance is slower, the problem appears later than the start of dosing.",
  "ch6.p3": "Taking all 3 doses at once, once a day, pushes the peak to 3.4 and lets the trough fall to 0.4: on the last day, only about 53% of the time is inside the window, 30% is below it and 17% above it. Split the same total into three doses and the peak is 2.0 and the trough 1.0, inside the window all day. The total has not changed; what has changed is the distance between peak and trough. The ratio of the midpoints of the two imaginary curves (the midpoint of harm ÷ the midpoint of effect) is called the <strong>therapeutic index</strong>; here it is 5 ÷ 0.8 ≈ 6.",
  "ch6.p4": "Now push “slower clearance” to ×2 (the half-life becomes 16 hours), still three doses a day. The first day looks normal, but the upper edge is first crossed at hour 24, and afterward the plateau holds at 2.4–3.4, above the window all day. The same prescription means different things for someone who clears slowly: the drug builds up gradually over several days, and the problem appears later than the start of treatment. This is also the “five half-lives” from chapter 2: a longer half-life means a longer time to reach the plateau.",
  "ch6.limit": "The two curves are imaginary and serve only to show the shape; the curves for real drugs must be measured in clinical studies. The model's “effect” and “harm” depend only on the concentration at a given moment. It leaves out delayed effects, interactions between drugs, and the fact that much harm depends on the cumulative total rather than on any single moment's concentration.",

  /* ───── 07 ───── */
  "ch7.title": "The heart's budget",
  "ch7.law": "To hold one quantity steady, another must change a lot. During exercise, what is held steady is the oxygen supply; heart rate is the quantity that gets adjusted.",
  "ch7.p1": "The volume of blood the heart pumps per minute is called <strong>cardiac output</strong>. It equals heart rate multiplied by the volume pumped per beat (<strong>stroke volume</strong>). For a 70 kg adult at rest, stroke volume is about 70 mL (normal range 55–100), heart rate is 60–100 beats per minute, and cardiac output averages about 5 L/min (range 4–8).",
  "ch7.p2": "When you start exercising, your muscles need several times more oxygen. The body keeps the supply adequate, and to do so it adjusts two numbers: stroke volume rises, and heart rate speeds up. In a healthy young person exercising hard, cardiac output can reach about 19.5 L/min, 4–5 times the resting value. This has the same structure as blood glucose in chapter 4: one quantity held steady, one quantity adjusted, with a delay in between.",
  "ch7.model.title": "Pushing up the exercise intensity",
  "ch7.model.how": "The top chart shows heart rate (gold) and the heart rate ceiling (red dashed line); the bottom chart shows cardiac output (blue) and the amount needed (grey dashed line). Drag the exercise intensity and watch heart rate catch up. Switch to “Trained”, then drag age upward. The numbers are illustrative; the shapes come from physiology textbooks.",
  "ch7.model.aria": "Heart rate and cardiac output over time during exercise",
