  "ch7.cv.co": "Cardiac output L/min",
  "ch7.cv.x": "Time →",
  "ch7.unit.level": "Level {v}",
  "ch7.unit.age": "{v} years old",
  "ch7.unit.bpm": "{v} bpm",
  "ch7.unit.ml": "{v} mL",
  "ch7.unit.lmin": "{v} / {need} L/min",
  "ch7.say.lagup": "Heart rate {hr}, still catching up. Until it does, the blood delivered is less than the {need} L/min needed.",
  "ch7.say.lagdown": "Intensity has dropped, and heart rate {hr} is falling back.",
  "ch7.say.ok": "Heart rate {hr} × stroke volume {sv} mL = {co} L/min, just meeting the need. There are {res} beats left before the heart rate ceiling of {hm}.",
  "ch7.say.short": "Heart rate has reached its ceiling of {hm}; cardiac output can reach at most {max} L/min, {short} L/min less than needed. Supply falls short, and this intensity cannot be sustained for long.",
  "ch7.p3": "The default is a 20-year-old who does not exercise regularly, jumping from rest straight to intensity level 6. Heart rate does not reach its target instantly; it takes tens of seconds to catch up with the roughly 131 bpm needed, and until it does, the blood delivered is less than the need.",
  "ch7.p4": "Switch to “Trained”: stroke volume is larger, so the same cardiac output needs fewer beats; resting heart rate drops from 71 to 56, and at intensity 6 from 131 to 100. The heart rate ceiling depends mainly on age; an analysis pooling 351 studies estimates it as 208 − 0.7 × age (more accurate than the common “220 − age”): about 194 at age 20 and about 166 at age 60. That is a population average; people of the same age differ considerably. Drag age to 60 and push the intensity to the maximum: an untrained person's cardiac output tops out at about 21.6 L/min against a need of 25, a shortfall of 3.4. At that point “holding steady” fails, and the only option is to lower the intensity. For how the extra blood is shared out to the muscles, see <a href=\"../blood-circulation/#step4\">step 4 of the Blood Circulation page</a>; for how blood pressure follows from cardiac output and vascular resistance, see <a href=\"../blood-circulation/#step3\">step 3</a>.",
  "ch7.limit": "The model has stroke volume rise linearly up to moderate intensity and then level off. It ignores the shorter filling time of the ventricles at very high heart rates, and it ignores blood pressure, the redistribution of blood, and the limits of the lungs. Stroke volume in trained people and the speed at which heart rate catches up are illustrative; in the model an untrained 20-year-old can pump up to about 25 L/min, more than the textbook's typical exercise value of about 19.5 L/min.",

  /* ───── 08 ───── */
  "ch8.title": "Antibiotics: concentration selects the bacteria",
  "ch8.law": "Drug concentration does more than kill bacteria; it also selects among them. A concentration that falls between what sensitive and resistant bacteria can tolerate leaves the resistant ones behind.",
  "ch8.p1": "A single infection can involve hundreds of millions of bacteria, and they are not all identical: now and then, one carries a mutation that makes the drug less effective. To suppress a given type of bacterium, the antibiotic concentration must reach at least its <strong>minimum inhibitory concentration</strong> (MIC). Resistant bacteria have a higher MIC.",
  "ch8.p2": "This creates a dangerous range of concentrations: higher than the MIC for sensitive bacteria, but lower than the MIC for resistant ones. Within this range, the drug kills off the sensitive bacteria and makes room for the resistant ones. Researchers call this the <strong>mutant selection window</strong>, and it has also been observed in infected rabbits. This is <a href=\"../../ecology/natural-selection/#step1\">natural selection</a>, only much faster.",
  "ch8.model.title": "One infection, two kinds of bacteria",
  "ch8.model.how": "The top chart shows the numbers of the two bacterial types (log scale, each gridline 10 times the last; pale gold background marks dosing days). The bottom chart shows drug concentration, using the chapter 2 model (one dose every 8 hours, half-life 4 hours). The purple band is the mutant selection window: the resistant bacteria's MIC is 8 times that of the sensitive ones. At the start, one resistant bacterium exists per million. Drag the dose and the course length.",
  "ch8.model.aria": "Numbers of two bacterial types and drug concentration over time",
  "ch8.leg.s": "Sensitive bacteria",
  "ch8.leg.r": "Resistant bacteria",
  "ch8.leg.c": "Drug concentration",
  "ch8.leg.win": "Mutant selection window",
  "ch8.ctl.dose": "Dose",
  "ch8.ctl.days": "Course length",
  "ch8.read.s": "Sensitive bacteria now",
  "ch8.read.r": "Resistant bacteria now",
